1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,304
Welcome to episode one of the System Design Workshop. By the end you'll be

2
00:00:04,304 --> 00:00:08,608
able to size a system with real numbers, explain latency, percentiles and the nines,

3
00:00:08,608 --> 00:00:12,298
scale a service out, and run a design interview step by step.

4
00:00:12,648 --> 00:00:16,971
Keep the Part 1 notes open. Each concept here matches a section there, in

5
00:00:16,971 --> 00:00:17,898
the same order.

6
00:00:18,548 --> 00:00:23,319
System design is deciding which parts software needs, what each does, how they talk,

7
00:00:23,319 --> 00:00:28,091
and where the data lives, so the whole meets its goals for users, speed,

8
00:00:28,091 --> 00:00:29,113
uptime and cost.

9
00:00:29,463 --> 00:00:34,574
Coding asks how one function works. Design asks how twenty programs on two hundred

10
00:00:34,574 --> 00:00:39,685
machines cooperate without losing data. Like an architect: floors and fire exits, not bricks.

11
00:00:40,035 --> 00:00:44,544
A program that works for ten users can collapse at ten million because its

12
00:00:44,544 --> 00:00:49,053
shape is wrong. One database everyone queues for, one machine whose death kills the

13
00:00:49,053 --> 00:00:51,630
site. Better loops don't fix that. Design does.

14
00:00:51,980 --> 00:00:55,602
Two working designs can also differ ten times over in their cloud bill.

15
00:00:56,252 --> 00:01:00,796
Design runs as a loop. Understand the goal: what it must do, and how

16
00:01:00,796 --> 00:01:05,339
well. Estimate the load. Sketch the parts. Find the weak spots: what runs out

17
00:01:05,339 --> 00:01:09,559
first, and what takes everything down. Then fix it and state the cost.

18
00:01:09,909 --> 00:01:14,188
You repeat as the system grows. A design is right for a given load,

19
00:01:14,188 --> 00:01:14,799
never finished.

20
00:01:15,449 --> 00:01:19,898
Your main tool is abstraction: a simple contract hiding the details. A remote call

21
00:01:19,898 --> 00:01:24,348
that looks local. A database that looks like one table but is many machines.

22
00:01:24,698 --> 00:01:30,620
Abstractions leak. Networks fail halfway, replicas lag, clocks drift. Good design names the leaks

23
00:01:30,620 --> 00:01:31,889
it must handle.

24
00:01:32,239 --> 00:01:36,670
And every choice gives something up. More copies are safer but cost more and

25
00:01:36,670 --> 00:01:38,886
can disagree. Say the trade-off out loud.

26
00:01:39,236 --> 00:01:44,498
In the interview there's rarely one right answer. They grade how you turn a

27
00:01:44,498 --> 00:01:49,760
vague idea into an architecture: asking questions, estimating scale, choosing parts, and explaining trade-offs.

28
00:01:49,760 --> 00:01:55,022
Coding is correctness inside one program; design is how many programs behave under load

29
00:01:55,022 --> 00:01:55,774
and failure.

30
00:01:56,423 --> 00:02:01,268
A client is any program that asks: a browser, an app, or another server.

31
00:02:01,268 --> 00:02:06,113
A server waits for requests and answers them. On the web they speak H-T-T-P,

32
00:02:06,113 --> 00:02:08,190
a standard request and response format.

33
00:02:08,540 --> 00:02:13,536
Open example dot com slash profile. The browser finds the server's address through D-N-S,

34
00:02:13,536 --> 00:02:18,533
the internet's phone book. It sends GET slash profile. The server looks up user

35
00:02:18,533 --> 00:02:22,816
forty-two in the database and replies two hundred, O-K, with the page.

36
00:02:23,166 --> 00:02:27,783
Inside big systems, servers are constantly clients of other servers. And a server is

37
00:02:27,783 --> 00:02:32,399
a role, not a box. One name like api dot example dot com is

38
00:02:32,399 --> 00:02:35,037
often hundreds of machines behind a load balancer.

39
00:02:35,687 --> 00:02:39,907
A thin client, like a basic web page, relies on the server. A thick

40
00:02:39,907 --> 00:02:42,621
client, like a mobile game, does a lot locally.

41
00:02:42,971 --> 00:02:48,718
Normally the client pulls. Chat needs the server to push, using WebSockets or server-sent

42
00:02:48,718 --> 00:02:50,360
events. That's episode two.

43
00:02:50,710 --> 00:02:55,066
And the network between them is slow and unreliable compared with work inside one

44
00:02:55,066 --> 00:02:55,377
machine.

45
00:02:55,727 --> 00:03:00,106
Recap: almost everything on the web is a chain of request and response, often

46
00:03:00,106 --> 00:03:01,045
server to server.

47
00:03:01,695 --> 00:03:06,655
Nearly every web app uses the same parts. The client renders the interface.

48
00:03:07,005 --> 00:03:10,975
The load balancer spreads requests over app servers and skips dead ones.

49
00:03:11,325 --> 00:03:16,183
App servers apply business rules. You run identical copies so one crash doesn't stop

50
00:03:16,183 --> 00:03:16,877
the site.

51
00:03:17,227 --> 00:03:22,228
The database stores data durably. It's usually the hardest part to scale, because it

52
00:03:22,228 --> 00:03:22,942
holds state.

53
00:03:23,292 --> 00:03:27,689
A cache keeps hot data in RAM. A hit is well under a millisecond,

54
00:03:27,689 --> 00:03:30,516
versus several milliseconds or more for a database query.

55
00:03:30,866 --> 00:03:35,417
A queue lets slow jobs, like welcome emails, run later on workers, so the

56
00:03:35,417 --> 00:03:36,718
request returns at once.

57
00:03:37,068 --> 00:03:42,318
Object storage holds big files by key. Databases are bad at large files.

58
00:03:42,668 --> 00:03:48,276
A C-D-N, content delivery network, keeps static files near users, so Sydney doesn't fetch

59
00:03:48,276 --> 00:03:49,878
every image from Virginia.

60
00:03:50,528 --> 00:03:54,634
Users get static files from the C-D-N and send the rest to the load

61
00:03:54,634 --> 00:03:58,740
balancer. App servers read the cache first, then the database, and drop slow jobs

62
00:03:58,740 --> 00:04:01,966
on the queue for a worker, which writes to object storage.

63
00:04:02,316 --> 00:04:06,707
Instagram's early stack looked almost exactly like this. Add a box only when you

64
00:04:06,707 --> 00:04:11,097
can name its problem: we add a cache because reads outnumber writes a hundred

65
00:04:11,097 --> 00:04:15,487
to one. A small internal tool may need just one server and one database.

66
00:04:16,137 --> 00:04:20,549
Don't store images in the database. Put files in object storage and keep only

67
00:04:20,549 --> 00:04:21,179
the key.

68
00:04:21,529 --> 00:04:26,373
Don't do slow work, like video encoding, inside the web request. Queue it.

69
00:04:26,723 --> 00:04:31,388
Interview question: why not one server? Because it's a single point of failure with

70
00:04:31,388 --> 00:04:36,053
a hard capacity limit. Stateless app servers behind a load balancer let you add

71
00:04:36,053 --> 00:04:37,386
machines and survive crashes.

72
00:04:37,736 --> 00:04:42,305
And add a queue when work is slow, can wait, or arrives in bursts.

73
00:04:42,305 --> 00:04:44,263
Recap: each box solves one problem.

74
00:04:44,913 --> 00:04:50,117
Three kinds of speed. Latency is how long one request takes, in milliseconds or

75
00:04:50,117 --> 00:04:50,489
microseconds.

76
00:04:50,839 --> 00:04:53,289
Throughput is how many requests finish per second.

77
00:04:53,639 --> 00:04:57,427
Bandwidth is the most a link can carry per second, in bits. The width

78
00:04:57,427 --> 00:04:58,239
of the pipe.

79
00:04:58,589 --> 00:05:03,225
On a motorway, latency is one car's journey time, bandwidth is the number of

80
00:05:03,225 --> 00:05:06,205
lanes, and throughput is cars actually arriving per hour.

81
00:05:06,555 --> 00:05:12,176
Strictly, response time is what the client feels: network both ways, plus queueing, plus

82
00:05:12,176 --> 00:05:16,995
service time, the actual work. In interviews latency usually means response time.

83
00:05:17,345 --> 00:05:22,501
Why care? Under about a hundred milliseconds feels instant; over a second breaks concentration.

84
00:05:22,501 --> 00:05:27,658
And throughput sets fleet size: fifty thousand requests a second at a thousand per

85
00:05:27,658 --> 00:05:32,814
server needs at least fifty servers. Also, latency and bandwidth are independent. A satellite

86
00:05:32,814 --> 00:05:37,970
link has huge bandwidth and six hundred millisecond round trips. More bandwidth won't speed

87
00:05:37,970 --> 00:05:39,812
up a small A-P-I call.

88
00:05:40,462 --> 00:05:45,232
Four delays. Propagation: light in fibre covers about two hundred kilometres per millisecond, so

89
00:05:45,232 --> 00:05:50,003
London to New York is at least twenty-eight milliseconds one way, about seventy for

90
00:05:50,003 --> 00:05:51,366
a real round trip.

91
00:05:51,716 --> 00:05:56,896
Transmission: one megabyte is eight megabits, so eighty milliseconds at a hundred megabits per

92
00:05:56,896 --> 00:06:02,076
second, eight at one gigabit. Then queueing behind other requests, and processing, the real

93
00:06:02,076 --> 00:06:02,446
work.

94
00:06:02,796 --> 00:06:07,430
Load links them. At low load latency is flat. Near full use, requests wait

95
00:06:07,430 --> 00:06:12,063
for each other and latency explodes while throughput levels off. That's why teams run

96
00:06:12,063 --> 00:06:14,379
at fifty to seventy percent at peak.

97
00:06:15,029 --> 00:06:19,559
Start with a latency budget. For a p ninety-nine of two hundred milliseconds, give

98
00:06:19,559 --> 00:06:24,088
sixty to the network, twenty to the gateway, eighty to the service and database,

99
00:06:24,088 --> 00:06:26,029
forty as slack. Fix whoever overspends.

100
00:06:26,379 --> 00:06:31,482
Biggest payoff first: remove round trips by batching and running independent calls in parallel.

101
00:06:31,482 --> 00:06:34,033
Reuse connections so you don't repeat handshakes.

102
00:06:34,383 --> 00:06:39,962
Move bytes closer with C-D-Ns and regional replicas. Serve hot reads from memory. Push

103
00:06:39,962 --> 00:06:42,353
emails and thumbnails onto a queue.

104
00:06:42,703 --> 00:06:48,000
Then shrink payloads, index queries, keep headroom, and cut the tail with deadlines and

105
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:49,892
hedged requests. Measure every hop.

106
00:06:50,242 --> 00:06:55,148
Fix the biggest term first: a seventy millisecond ocean crossing dwarfs a two millisecond

107
00:06:55,148 --> 00:06:58,302
parse. And name each lever's price, like stale caches.

108
00:06:58,652 --> 00:07:03,234
Pause and think: a user in Australia is slow against servers in Virginia. Will

109
00:07:03,234 --> 00:07:04,216
more bandwidth help?

110
00:07:04,866 --> 00:07:10,713
Barely. The round trip is about two hundred milliseconds. Cut round trips: a nearby

111
00:07:10,713 --> 00:07:14,055
C-D-N, fewer sequential calls, maybe a regional deployment.

112
00:07:14,405 --> 00:07:18,673
Don't mix bits and bytes. A hundred megabits per second moves at most twelve

113
00:07:18,673 --> 00:07:20,502
and a half megabytes per second.

114
00:07:20,852 --> 00:07:25,554
Batching raises throughput but adds latency: the first write waits for the other ninety-nine.

115
00:07:25,904 --> 00:07:30,792
Recap: a cargo ship has high throughput and high latency; a motorbike courier, the

116
00:07:30,792 --> 00:07:31,141
reverse.

117
00:07:31,791 --> 00:07:36,584
A percentile is the value below which that percentage of measurements fall. A p

118
00:07:36,584 --> 00:07:41,378
ninety-five of three hundred milliseconds means ninety-five percent finished within three hundred. p fifty

119
00:07:41,378 --> 00:07:42,405
is the median.

120
00:07:42,755 --> 00:07:45,530
p ninety: one in ten requests is slower.

121
00:07:45,880 --> 00:07:47,911
p ninety-five: one in twenty.

122
00:07:48,261 --> 00:07:50,096
p ninety-nine: one in a hundred.

123
00:07:50,446 --> 00:07:55,081
p ninety-nine point nine: one in a thousand. This slow end is tail latency.

124
00:07:55,731 --> 00:08:00,700
Ten requests: nine take ten milliseconds, one takes a thousand. The mean is a

125
00:08:00,700 --> 00:08:03,895
hundred and nine milliseconds, which matches no real request.

126
00:08:04,245 --> 00:08:09,358
The median is ten. But p ninety-five and p ninety-nine are a full second:

127
00:08:09,358 --> 00:08:14,471
one user in ten waits. Real latency is skewed by garbage collection, cache misses

128
00:08:14,471 --> 00:08:17,758
and retries. Percentiles show that shape. Averages flatten it.

129
00:08:18,408 --> 00:08:22,560
The tail matters more than it looks. A user loading a hundred pages a

130
00:08:22,560 --> 00:08:26,712
day hits your p ninety-nine daily, and heavy users are often your best customers.

131
00:08:27,062 --> 00:08:31,379
Worse, fan-out. One request calls a hundred servers and waits for all. Each is

132
00:08:31,379 --> 00:08:35,696
slow just one percent of the time. The chance at least one is slow

133
00:08:35,696 --> 00:08:40,013
is one minus point nine nine to the hundredth, about sixty-three percent. That's from

134
00:08:40,013 --> 00:08:41,863
Google's paper, The Tail at Scale.

135
00:08:42,513 --> 00:08:47,274
Monitoring uses histograms, counts per latency bucket, to estimate percentiles.

136
00:08:47,624 --> 00:08:51,922
Never average percentiles across servers. Merge the histograms instead.

137
00:08:52,272 --> 00:08:56,764
Cut the tail with hedged requests: no reply by the p ninety-five time, ask

138
00:08:56,764 --> 00:09:00,936
a second replica and take the first answer. Add timeouts and limit fan-out.

139
00:09:01,286 --> 00:09:06,359
Beware coordinated omission: a load tester that waits on slow responses sends less traffic

140
00:09:06,359 --> 00:09:08,170
exactly then, hiding the tail.

141
00:09:08,520 --> 00:09:13,826
Interview question: your service's p ninety-nine is fine but users still complain. Why? Each

142
00:09:13,826 --> 00:09:19,131
page calls many services, so the chance of at least one slow call per

143
00:09:19,131 --> 00:09:24,437
page is far above one percent. Look at end-to-end latency. Recap: percentiles, not averages.

144
00:09:25,087 --> 00:09:30,419
Little's law holds for any stable system. L equals lambda times W. L is

145
00:09:30,419 --> 00:09:35,752
items inside at once, like requests in flight. Lambda is the arrival rate, which

146
00:09:35,752 --> 00:09:39,180
equals throughput. W is time spent inside, the latency.

147
00:09:39,530 --> 00:09:43,756
Two customers arrive at a café per minute and each stays ten minutes, so

148
00:09:43,756 --> 00:09:46,473
twenty are inside on average, whatever the barista does.

149
00:09:47,123 --> 00:09:51,456
A thousand requests per second at point two seconds each is two hundred in

150
00:09:51,456 --> 00:09:55,171
flight. You need at least two hundred threads or connections, plus headroom.

151
00:09:55,521 --> 00:10:00,427
Twenty database connections with five millisecond queries serve at most twenty divided by point

152
00:10:00,427 --> 00:10:03,230
zero zero five: four thousand queries per second.

153
00:10:03,580 --> 00:10:09,149
With a fixed concurrency limit, if latency doubles, max throughput halves. That's why slow

154
00:10:09,149 --> 00:10:09,945
dependencies hurt.

155
00:10:10,295 --> 00:10:15,374
It needs a stable system, and convert milliseconds to seconds first. Interview example: five

156
00:10:15,374 --> 00:10:20,454
thousand requests a second at forty milliseconds is two hundred in flight, about four

157
00:10:20,454 --> 00:10:22,268
per instance across fifty instances.

158
00:10:22,618 --> 00:10:27,978
Pause: a hundred threads, each held two hundred and fifty milliseconds. Max throughput? A

159
00:10:27,978 --> 00:10:32,189
hundred divided by point two five: four hundred requests per second.

160
00:10:32,839 --> 00:10:38,190
From Jeff Dean and Peter Norvig. Learn orders of magnitude, not digits. An L

161
00:10:38,190 --> 00:10:40,101
one cache read: one nanosecond.

162
00:10:40,451 --> 00:10:42,994
A RAM read: a hundred nanoseconds.

163
00:10:43,344 --> 00:10:48,533
A random four kilobyte read from an N-V-M-e S-S-D: twenty to a hundred microseconds.

164
00:10:48,883 --> 00:10:52,262
A round trip inside one data centre: half a millisecond.

165
00:10:52,612 --> 00:10:56,745
One megabyte read sequentially from S-S-D: point two to one millisecond.

166
00:10:57,095 --> 00:11:01,054
A hard disk seek: ten milliseconds, because an arm physically moves.

167
00:11:01,404 --> 00:11:04,585
London to New York round trip: seventy milliseconds.

168
00:11:04,935 --> 00:11:07,362
California to Europe: a hundred and fifty.

169
00:11:08,012 --> 00:11:11,972
Scale it up. If an L one read took a second, RAM would take

170
00:11:11,972 --> 00:11:15,933
about two minutes, a data-centre round trip about six days, a disk seek four

171
00:11:15,933 --> 00:11:18,196
months, and London to New York two years.

172
00:11:18,846 --> 00:11:23,149
RAM is a hundred to a thousand times faster than S-S-D for random reads,

173
00:11:23,149 --> 00:11:24,686
so caches live in RAM.

174
00:11:25,036 --> 00:11:29,475
A thousand sequential database calls cost at least half a second in round trips

175
00:11:29,475 --> 00:11:30,426
alone. Batch them.

176
00:11:30,776 --> 00:11:35,828
Ocean crossings cost seventy to a hundred and fifty milliseconds. Only moving data closer

177
00:11:35,828 --> 00:11:36,189
helps.

178
00:11:36,539 --> 00:11:40,428
Sequential beats random, so storage engines write append-only logs.

179
00:11:40,778 --> 00:11:45,325
A remote Redis isn't free: the read is nanoseconds, but the trip is half

180
00:11:45,325 --> 00:11:49,872
a millisecond. Interview question: why is a service making fifty calls per request slow,

181
00:11:49,872 --> 00:11:54,419
when each call is fast? Fifty sequential calls at about a millisecond each is

182
00:11:54,419 --> 00:11:57,342
fifty milliseconds before any real work. Batch or parallelise.

183
00:11:57,992 --> 00:12:01,933
A byte is eight bits. Each unit is about a thousand times the last.

184
00:12:01,933 --> 00:12:05,874
Two to the ten is ten twenty-four, but estimates round it to a thousand.

185
00:12:05,874 --> 00:12:07,845
A kilobyte: a short post with metadata.

186
00:12:08,195 --> 00:12:11,039
A megabyte: a photo, or a minute of M-P-three.

187
00:12:11,389 --> 00:12:15,174
A gigabyte: an hour of streamed H-D video is a few of these.

188
00:12:15,524 --> 00:12:17,777
A terabyte: a laptop drive.

189
00:12:18,127 --> 00:12:20,437
A petabyte: a large data warehouse.

190
00:12:21,087 --> 00:12:25,256
Two to the thirty-two is about four point three billion: the I-P version four

191
00:12:25,256 --> 00:12:27,937
address space, and where thirty-two bit IDs run out.

192
00:12:28,287 --> 00:12:33,623
A timestamp is eight bytes, a U-U-I-D sixteen. A compressed photo is two hundred

193
00:12:33,623 --> 00:12:38,579
kilobytes to two megabytes. A minute of H-D video, twenty-five to sixty megabytes.

194
00:12:38,929 --> 00:12:42,854
A day is eighty-six thousand four hundred seconds. Round it to ten to the

195
00:12:42,854 --> 00:12:43,134
five.

196
00:12:43,484 --> 00:12:46,050
So one million per day is about twelve per second.

197
00:12:46,400 --> 00:12:50,572
And a one gigabit card moves a hundred and twenty-five megabytes per second at

198
00:12:50,572 --> 00:12:50,870
best.

199
00:12:51,520 --> 00:12:56,898
A back-of-the-envelope estimate finds the order of magnitude, and that decides the architecture. A

200
00:12:56,898 --> 00:13:02,276
hundred requests per second fits one server. A hundred thousand needs caching and sharding.

201
00:13:02,626 --> 00:13:07,792
D-A-U is daily active users. Q-P-S is queries per second. Requests per day divided

202
00:13:07,792 --> 00:13:12,958
by ten to the five gives average Q-P-S. Peak is usually two to three

203
00:13:12,958 --> 00:13:15,172
times that, more for spiky products.

204
00:13:15,522 --> 00:13:21,335
Storage per day is items times size, times days kept, times replicas. Bandwidth is

205
00:13:21,335 --> 00:13:25,071
Q-P-S times response size. State every assumption out loud.

206
00:13:25,721 --> 00:13:31,215
A photo app: ten million daily users, each viewing fifty photos and uploading point

207
00:13:31,215 --> 00:13:36,709
two a day. Photos average five hundred kilobytes, metadata one kilobyte, kept five years.

208
00:13:36,709 --> 00:13:42,203
Reads: five hundred million a day, so about five thousand Q-P-S, fifteen thousand at

209
00:13:42,203 --> 00:13:42,596
peak.

210
00:13:42,946 --> 00:13:47,683
Writes: two million a day, about twenty per second, sixty at peak. Two hundred

211
00:13:47,683 --> 00:13:50,390
and fifty reads per write, so caching matters.

212
00:13:50,740 --> 00:13:55,368
Storage: two million times five hundred kilobytes is one terabyte of photos a day,

213
00:13:55,368 --> 00:13:57,021
plus two gigabytes of metadata.

214
00:13:57,371 --> 00:14:02,747
Over five years, about one point eight petabytes, times three with replicas. Photos go

215
00:14:02,747 --> 00:14:03,899
in object storage.

216
00:14:04,249 --> 00:14:08,916
Bandwidth: five thousand views a second times five hundred kilobytes is two and a

217
00:14:08,916 --> 00:14:13,249
half gigabytes per second, about twenty gigabits. A strong case for a C-D-N.

218
00:14:13,899 --> 00:14:19,306
Now server count. Thirty thousand requests per second at peak. Each uses four milliseconds

219
00:14:19,306 --> 00:14:24,713
of C-P-U and takes fifty milliseconds end to end. Sixteen-core machines, three availability zones,

220
00:14:24,713 --> 00:14:30,120
must survive losing one. One core: a thousand divided by four, two hundred and

221
00:14:30,120 --> 00:14:31,278
fifty per second.

222
00:14:31,628 --> 00:14:34,972
Times sixteen cores: four thousand per server at full load.

223
00:14:35,322 --> 00:14:39,304
Keep C-P-U at sixty percent so queues stay short: twenty-four hundred.

224
00:14:39,654 --> 00:14:44,229
Thirty thousand divided by twenty-four hundred is twelve and a half. Round up to

225
00:14:44,229 --> 00:14:44,556
thirteen.

226
00:14:44,906 --> 00:14:49,629
Little's law check: thirty thousand times point zero five is fifteen hundred in flight,

227
00:14:49,629 --> 00:14:52,328
about a hundred and fifteen per server. Fine.

228
00:14:52,678 --> 00:14:58,115
Zone loss: thirteen must survive with two zones left, so seven per zone. Times

229
00:14:58,115 --> 00:14:58,892
three: twenty-one.

230
00:14:59,242 --> 00:15:03,468
Rolling deploys take about ten percent out: roughly twenty-three servers.

231
00:15:03,818 --> 00:15:09,240
Network: twenty kilobyte responses give about four point eight gigabits total, a quarter gigabit

232
00:15:09,240 --> 00:15:11,564
per server. C-P-U is the limit.

233
00:15:12,214 --> 00:15:16,508
The naive answer, thirty thousand over four thousand, was eight. The ceiling and zone-loss

234
00:15:16,508 --> 00:15:18,962
rule explain the gap of about three times.

235
00:15:19,312 --> 00:15:23,274
C-P-U per request set the number. Halve it and you halve the fleet.

236
00:15:23,624 --> 00:15:29,351
Repeat per tier: caches are limited by memory, databases by disk operations and writes,

237
00:15:29,351 --> 00:15:30,578
streams by bandwidth.

238
00:15:30,928 --> 00:15:36,400
Common mistakes: forgetting peak, replicas, and index overhead of twenty to fifty percent, and

239
00:15:36,400 --> 00:15:39,917
false precision. Spend three to five minutes, out loud.

240
00:15:40,267 --> 00:15:45,206
Say it in one breath: four milliseconds of C-P-U means four thousand a second

241
00:15:45,206 --> 00:15:50,146
per box; at sixty percent, twenty-four hundred; thirteen for peak; seven per zone across

242
00:15:50,146 --> 00:15:52,968
three zones, twenty-one, plus a little for deploys.

243
00:15:53,318 --> 00:15:57,948
Pause: a hundred million daily users, twenty requests each. Plan for what Q-P-S? Two

244
00:15:57,948 --> 00:16:02,577
billion a day over ten to the five is twenty thousand average, about sixty

245
00:16:02,577 --> 00:16:07,207
thousand at peak. For scale, WhatsApp's hundred billion messages a day is about a

246
00:16:07,207 --> 00:16:08,199
million per second.

247
00:16:08,849 --> 00:16:13,580
Availability is the share of time a system works correctly: uptime over total time,

248
00:16:13,580 --> 00:16:18,312
or successful requests over valid ones. Using a three sixty-five day year and thirty

249
00:16:18,312 --> 00:16:23,044
day month: ninety-nine percent allows three point six five days down a year, seven

250
00:16:23,044 --> 00:16:24,734
point two hours a month.

251
00:16:25,084 --> 00:16:29,391
Three nines, ninety-nine point nine: eight point seven six hours a year, forty-three minutes

252
00:16:29,391 --> 00:16:32,468
a month, about a minute and a half a day.

253
00:16:32,818 --> 00:16:37,338
Ninety-nine point nine five: four point three eight hours a year, about twenty-two minutes

254
00:16:37,338 --> 00:16:37,984
a month.

255
00:16:38,334 --> 00:16:42,640
Four nines: fifty-three minutes a year, four point three minutes a month. Less than

256
00:16:42,640 --> 00:16:46,638
a human needs to wake and log in, so recovery must be automatic.

257
00:16:46,988 --> 00:16:51,727
Five nines: about five minutes a year, twenty-six seconds a month. Each nine cuts

258
00:16:51,727 --> 00:16:56,128
downtime ten times, and usually costs far more than ten times the effort.

259
00:16:56,778 --> 00:17:01,578
Parts in series must all work, so multiply. A load balancer at four nines,

260
00:17:01,578 --> 00:17:06,377
app at three, database at three: about ninety-nine point seven nine percent. Lower than

261
00:17:06,377 --> 00:17:07,406
the weakest link.

262
00:17:07,756 --> 00:17:12,288
Parts in parallel need only one. Two independent servers at ninety-nine percent are both

263
00:17:12,288 --> 00:17:16,820
down one percent of one percent of the time: ninety-nine point nine nine. But

264
00:17:16,820 --> 00:17:20,381
only if failures are independent; one rack's power cut takes both.

265
00:17:21,031 --> 00:17:25,953
Three nines to four can mean multiple data centres and roughly double infrastructure. Many

266
00:17:25,953 --> 00:17:28,766
internal tools are fine at ninety-nine point five.

267
00:17:29,116 --> 00:17:33,950
Cloud S-L-As for single services are usually three or four nines. S-three is designed

268
00:17:33,950 --> 00:17:36,712
for four nines but its contract promises three.

269
00:17:37,062 --> 00:17:39,546
Planned maintenance still counts as downtime.

270
00:17:39,896 --> 00:17:45,128
Interview question: how do you beat any single machine's availability? Redundant copies in parallel

271
00:17:45,128 --> 00:17:50,359
across racks, zones or regions, with automatic failover and no single points of failure.

272
00:17:50,359 --> 00:17:54,844
Recap: three nines is forty-three minutes a month, four nines about four.

273
00:17:55,494 --> 00:18:00,857
An S-L-I, service level indicator, is a measurement: say, the percentage of requests succeeding

274
00:18:00,857 --> 00:18:03,539
under three hundred milliseconds over thirty days.

275
00:18:03,889 --> 00:18:09,242
An S-L-O, service level objective, is an internal target for it: ninety-nine point nine

276
00:18:09,242 --> 00:18:09,625
percent.

277
00:18:09,975 --> 00:18:15,954
An S-L-A, service level agreement, is a customer contract with penalties, usually service credits.

278
00:18:16,304 --> 00:18:21,561
Pizza shop: actual delivery times, the manager's ninety-five percent in thirty minutes, and the

279
00:18:21,561 --> 00:18:26,817
printed free-if-over-forty-five promise. The S-L-A is looser, so missing the S-L-O warns you before

280
00:18:26,817 --> 00:18:27,568
you pay.

281
00:18:28,218 --> 00:18:33,038
The error budget is one minus the S-L-O. At ninety-nine point nine, that's point

282
00:18:33,038 --> 00:18:37,858
one percent: of a hundred million monthly requests, a hundred thousand may fail, or

283
00:18:37,858 --> 00:18:39,924
about forty-three minutes of full outage.

284
00:18:40,274 --> 00:18:45,454
While budget remains, ship fast. When it's spent, freeze risky changes and fix reliability.

285
00:18:45,454 --> 00:18:50,634
This comes from Google's site reliability engineering. Pick indicators users feel, like failed checkouts,

286
00:18:50,634 --> 00:18:55,815
not C-P-U. And never set an S-L-O of a hundred percent: any change would

287
00:18:55,815 --> 00:19:00,995
violate it. Measure over a rolling twenty-eight or thirty days, and alert when the

288
00:19:00,995 --> 00:19:06,176
budget burns too fast. In the interview: the S-L-O is the internal target, the

289
00:19:06,176 --> 00:19:08,026
S-L-A the looser external contract.

290
00:19:08,676 --> 00:19:15,252
M-T-B-F, mean time between failures, should be high. M-T-T-R, mean time to repair or

291
00:19:15,252 --> 00:19:20,419
recover, should be low. Availability is M-T-B-F over M-T-B-F plus M-T-T-R.

292
00:19:20,769 --> 00:19:25,297
Fail every thousand hours, recover in one: about ninety-nine point nine percent.

293
00:19:25,946 --> 00:19:31,833
At scale something always fails, so cutting M-T-T-R is usually cheaper than raising M-T-B-F.

294
00:19:32,183 --> 00:19:37,825
Same server, but automatic ten-second recovery: about ninety-nine point nine nine nine seven percent.

295
00:19:38,175 --> 00:19:43,378
Detection counts. M-T-T-D, mean time to detect: an outage nobody notices for thirty minutes

296
00:19:43,378 --> 00:19:46,351
has at least thirty minutes of repair time.

297
00:19:46,701 --> 00:19:51,508
Recap: you can't prevent every failure, so recover fast with monitoring and automation.

298
00:19:52,158 --> 00:19:54,515
Availability: can I use it right now?

299
00:19:54,865 --> 00:19:59,882
Reliability: does it do the right thing over time? A system can be available

300
00:19:59,882 --> 00:20:02,391
but unreliable, answering fast with wrong data.

301
00:20:02,741 --> 00:20:08,639
Durability: will saved data survive crashes and dead disks? A system can be down

302
00:20:08,639 --> 00:20:14,538
but durable. S-three promises eleven nines durability but only four nines availability: separate promises,

303
00:20:14,538 --> 00:20:19,595
separate techniques. And replication isn't backup: a deleting bug is copied everywhere.

304
00:20:20,245 --> 00:20:24,345
Think of a bank. The cash machine works when you walk up: availability.

305
00:20:24,696 --> 00:20:28,434
It gives the right amount and debits the right account: reliability.

306
00:20:28,784 --> 00:20:34,897
Your balance survives a flood: durability. Availability comes from redundancy and failover; durability from

307
00:20:34,897 --> 00:20:39,700
copies in separate places, writing to disk before confirming, and backups.

308
00:20:40,050 --> 00:20:45,195
Pause: can a system be durable but not available? Yes. A database down for

309
00:20:45,195 --> 00:20:50,341
maintenance can't be read, but its data is safe on disk. Recap: usable now,

310
00:20:50,341 --> 00:20:52,179
correct over time, data survives.

311
00:20:52,829 --> 00:20:57,792
A fault is one part going wrong. A failure is the whole system not

312
00:20:57,792 --> 00:21:02,755
serving users. Fault tolerance stops faults becoming failures, like a twin-engine plane landing on

313
00:21:02,755 --> 00:21:05,236
one engine. Hardware faults: defend with redundancy.

314
00:21:05,586 --> 00:21:08,372
Software bugs: tests and gradual rollouts.

315
00:21:08,722 --> 00:21:12,031
Network faults: timeouts, retries, other zones.

316
00:21:12,381 --> 00:21:16,433
Human error, like a bad config: reviews, backups, rollback.

317
00:21:16,783 --> 00:21:20,347
Slow dependencies: timeouts, fallbacks, circuit breakers.

318
00:21:20,997 --> 00:21:26,851
Failure models describe how a broken part behaves; each row allows more misbehaviour. Fail-stop

319
00:21:26,851 --> 00:21:29,777
halts and everyone is told: an idealisation.

320
00:21:30,127 --> 00:21:34,800
Crash-stop halts silently, so it looks just like a slow node. Majority quorums need

321
00:21:34,800 --> 00:21:37,804
two f plus one nodes to survive f crashes.

322
00:21:38,154 --> 00:21:42,705
Crash-recovery restarts with only what it saved, so write durably before acknowledging.

323
00:21:43,055 --> 00:21:46,666
Omission drops some messages: use acknowledgements and retries.

324
00:21:47,016 --> 00:21:51,990
Timing gives right answers too late, like a long G-C pause: use timeouts and

325
00:21:51,990 --> 00:21:52,345
deadlines.

326
00:21:52,695 --> 00:21:58,206
Byzantine does anything, even lies, and needs three f plus one replicas. Most data

327
00:21:58,206 --> 00:22:03,717
centres assume crash-recovery, omission and timing, plus checksums for corrupted data. So Raft survives

328
00:22:03,717 --> 00:22:07,654
one crash with three nodes; a Byzantine protocol needs four.

329
00:22:08,304 --> 00:22:10,266
Detect with health checks and heartbeats.

330
00:22:10,616 --> 00:22:15,150
Isolate with timeouts, so one fault doesn't spread. A dependency that takes thirty seconds

331
00:22:15,150 --> 00:22:19,036
to fail can tie up every thread; a fast error is better.

332
00:22:19,386 --> 00:22:25,248
Recover by routing around, restarting or failing over. And remember correlated failure: one bad

333
00:22:25,248 --> 00:22:26,923
deploy breaks every copy.

334
00:22:27,573 --> 00:22:29,919
Redundancy means a spare for each critical part.

335
00:22:30,269 --> 00:22:35,686
A single point of failure, a SPOF, is any part whose loss stops everything:

336
00:22:35,686 --> 00:22:41,103
one database, one load balancer, one D-N-S provider. Ask of every box: if this

337
00:22:41,103 --> 00:22:42,264
disappears, what happens?

338
00:22:42,614 --> 00:22:46,271
Failover moves work to the spare, like a hospital's backup generator.

339
00:22:46,621 --> 00:22:51,482
Run two or more copies in separate failure domains, such as availability zones, with

340
00:22:51,482 --> 00:22:53,913
health checks and automatic failover in seconds.

341
00:22:54,563 --> 00:23:00,431
Active-passive: a primary serves everything and replicates to an idle standby. Simple, but failover

342
00:23:00,431 --> 00:23:06,299
takes seconds to minutes. Active-active: all nodes serve, and survivors absorb a failure. Better,

343
00:23:06,299 --> 00:23:09,652
but hard for databases, where two writers conflict.

344
00:23:10,002 --> 00:23:15,392
Standbys are cold, warm or hot: ready in hours, minutes, or seconds. Run N

345
00:23:15,392 --> 00:23:20,782
plus one to survive a failure, N plus two to survive one during maintenance.

346
00:23:20,782 --> 00:23:26,172
Beware split brain: two primaries both accepting writes. And test failover, or it fails

347
00:23:26,172 --> 00:23:31,562
when needed. Real example: Amazon R-D-S Multi-A-Z keeps a synchronous standby in another zone

348
00:23:31,562 --> 00:23:36,952
and fails over in a minute or two. In the interview: active-passive suits single-writer

349
00:23:36,952 --> 00:23:38,877
databases; active-active suits stateless services.

350
00:23:39,527 --> 00:23:44,833
Scalability is handling more load by adding resources, without a redesign and without collapsing.

351
00:23:45,183 --> 00:23:50,755
Always name the load parameter: requests per second, data size, users. Say: we scale

352
00:23:50,755 --> 00:23:53,940
to ten times the requests by adding servers.

353
00:23:54,290 --> 00:23:59,386
Dimensions: load, data, geography, and organisation, meaning more engineers.

354
00:23:59,737 --> 00:24:05,038
Techniques: stateless servers, caches, read replicas, sharding, queues and C-D-Ns. More app servers don't

355
00:24:05,038 --> 00:24:10,339
help if the database is the bottleneck. Make the next ten times easy, not

356
00:24:10,339 --> 00:24:11,096
a thousand.

357
00:24:11,746 --> 00:24:18,207
Vertical scaling, scaling up, makes one machine bigger: a bigger bus. Horizontal scaling, scaling

358
00:24:18,207 --> 00:24:20,514
out, adds machines: more buses.

359
00:24:20,864 --> 00:24:26,086
Scaling up is often just a config change. Scaling out needs divisible work. Stateless

360
00:24:26,086 --> 00:24:29,069
app servers are easy. Database writes need sharding.

361
00:24:29,719 --> 00:24:31,809
Up is simple, with no code change.

362
00:24:32,159 --> 00:24:34,353
But it has a ceiling, the biggest machine.

363
00:24:34,703 --> 00:24:38,767
And it's still a SPOF, with big boxes costing disproportionately more.

364
00:24:39,117 --> 00:24:41,009
Out is practically unlimited.

365
00:24:41,359 --> 00:24:44,714
Losing a node loses only a fraction, and nodes add live.

366
00:24:45,064 --> 00:24:50,214
But it needs statelessness or sharding, and consistency gets harder. Stack Overflow in twenty

367
00:24:50,214 --> 00:24:55,363
sixteen ran on eleven web servers and four database servers, so don't dismiss scaling

368
00:24:55,363 --> 00:24:57,202
up. Most systems do both.

369
00:24:57,852 --> 00:25:02,712
Elasticity is scaling up and down automatically as load changes, so you pay for

370
00:25:02,712 --> 00:25:07,572
what you use. Scalability says we can grow; elasticity says we do it ourselves.

371
00:25:07,922 --> 00:25:13,694
Autoscaling watches a metric: above seventy percent C-P-U for five minutes, add servers; below

372
00:25:13,694 --> 00:25:16,992
thirty, remove some. Serverless can scale to zero.

373
00:25:17,342 --> 00:25:23,372
Limits: containers take seconds and V-Ms minutes to start, so spikes can outrun you.

374
00:25:23,372 --> 00:25:29,402
Mostly stateless parts are elastic. Over-eager scale-down causes flapping. In the interview: scalability is

375
00:25:29,402 --> 00:25:33,709
the capability; elasticity is doing it automatically, in both directions.

376
00:25:34,359 --> 00:25:38,760
State is data remembered between requests, like logins or carts. A stateless service keeps

377
00:25:38,760 --> 00:25:43,162
none in memory, so any copy serves any request, like a call centre. A

378
00:25:43,162 --> 00:25:47,564
stateful one ties you to a server, like a family doctor who's away on

379
00:25:47,564 --> 00:25:47,878
holiday.

380
00:25:48,228 --> 00:25:52,613
To go stateless, keep sessions in Redis or a signed token, files in object

381
00:25:52,613 --> 00:25:56,998
storage, and carts in a database. If you must stay stateful, like a game

382
00:25:56,998 --> 00:26:00,131
server, use sticky sessions and plan for that server dying.

383
00:26:00,481 --> 00:26:06,049
Cost: one extra hop, usually under a millisecond. Classic bug: local sessions, a second

384
00:26:06,049 --> 00:26:10,424
server, random logouts. And the state store itself still needs scaling.

385
00:26:11,074 --> 00:26:12,851
Any copy handles any request.

386
00:26:13,201 --> 00:26:16,417
Add, remove or kill copies freely without losing data.

387
00:26:16,767 --> 00:26:21,750
State lives in stores built for replication. Recap: keep app servers stateless.

388
00:26:22,400 --> 00:26:25,256
Performance is speed for one request at current load.

389
00:26:25,606 --> 00:26:27,498
It's about latency and efficiency.

390
00:26:27,848 --> 00:26:32,260
Diagnostic: if one request to an idle system is slow, it's performance.

391
00:26:32,610 --> 00:26:35,687
Scalability is whether that speed holds as load grows.

392
00:26:36,037 --> 00:26:38,974
Fast alone but slow under traffic means scalability.

393
00:26:39,324 --> 00:26:45,246
Pause: slow at three a.m. with no traffic? Performance. Profile the request, maybe an

394
00:26:45,246 --> 00:26:47,784
unindexed query. More servers won't help.

395
00:26:48,434 --> 00:26:52,103
Concurrency is many tasks in progress, interleaved while each waits.

396
00:26:52,453 --> 00:26:55,286
It's about organisation, and one core is enough.

397
00:26:55,636 --> 00:26:59,072
Rob Pike: concurrency is dealing with lots of things at once.

398
00:26:59,422 --> 00:27:02,011
Parallelism is tasks running at the same instant.

399
00:27:02,361 --> 00:27:05,241
It's about execution, and needs multiple cores.

400
00:27:05,591 --> 00:27:11,008
Parallelism is doing lots at once. One barista juggling orders is concurrent; three baristas

401
00:27:11,008 --> 00:27:11,781
is parallel.

402
00:27:12,431 --> 00:27:18,110
Web servers mostly wait on I-O. An event loop sends query A, doesn't wait,

403
00:27:18,110 --> 00:27:23,789
starts request B, then finishes each as results arrive. Threads, processes and goroutines are

404
00:27:23,789 --> 00:27:27,439
other routes to concurrency. Parallelism speeds up C-P-U-heavy work.

405
00:27:27,789 --> 00:27:32,316
Hazards: a race condition, where two withdrawals both read a hundred and write ninety,

406
00:27:32,316 --> 00:27:36,842
losing one. Deadlock, where two tasks wait on each other's locks. And Amdahl's law:

407
00:27:36,842 --> 00:27:41,368
if ten percent of a job is serial, infinite cores give at most ten

408
00:27:41,368 --> 00:27:41,692
times.

409
00:27:42,342 --> 00:27:45,442
Use concurrency for I-O-bound work that mostly waits.

410
00:27:45,792 --> 00:27:50,713
Use parallelism for C-P-U-bound work, but no more C-P-U threads than cores, or you

411
00:27:50,713 --> 00:27:52,119
only add switching overhead.

412
00:27:52,469 --> 00:27:58,599
Interview question: is single-threaded Node concurrent? Yes, via its event loop. But not parallel

413
00:27:58,599 --> 00:28:04,729
for JavaScript; run several processes for more cores. Recap: concurrency juggles, parallelism does at

414
00:28:04,729 --> 00:28:05,167
once.

415
00:28:05,817 --> 00:28:11,198
A bottleneck is the part that limits capacity; fixing anything else won't help. C-P-U:

416
00:28:11,198 --> 00:28:14,657
near a hundred percent. Optimise, cache, or add cores.

417
00:28:15,007 --> 00:28:19,723
Memory: swapping or out-of-memory crashes. Add RAM or evict wisely.

418
00:28:20,073 --> 00:28:24,534
Disk: high wait times. Use S-S-Ds, caching, sharding.

419
00:28:24,884 --> 00:28:29,856
Network: saturated links. Compress, use C-D-Ns, make fewer calls.

420
00:28:30,506 --> 00:28:35,873
Disks have two limits. I-O-P-S counts small random operations, like lookups. Throughput counts megabytes

421
00:28:35,873 --> 00:28:41,240
per second for big sequential transfers. A hard disk does about seventy-five to a

422
00:28:41,240 --> 00:28:44,690
hundred and fifty I-O-P-S, since the arm must move.

423
00:28:45,040 --> 00:28:49,579
A SATA S-S-D: tens of thousands, at about five hundred megabytes a second.

424
00:28:49,929 --> 00:28:52,867
N-V-M-e: hundreds of thousands to about a million.

425
00:28:53,217 --> 00:28:57,862
A cloud gp3 disk starts at three thousand I-O-P-S and a hundred and twenty-five

426
00:28:57,862 --> 00:29:02,508
megabytes per second. Twenty thousand random reads a second needs over a hundred hard

427
00:29:02,508 --> 00:29:04,167
disks, or one N-V-M-e drive.

428
00:29:04,817 --> 00:29:07,525
Measure each resource under load. Don't guess.

429
00:29:07,875 --> 00:29:13,340
Look for limits and queues: full thread pools, exhausted connections, lock waits. Also hot

430
00:29:13,340 --> 00:29:14,902
rows and rate limits.

431
00:29:15,252 --> 00:29:18,145
Fix, then re-measure. The bottleneck moves.

432
00:29:18,495 --> 00:29:24,336
Pause: latency rises but C-P-U is at thirty percent? Look elsewhere: disk, network, a

433
00:29:24,336 --> 00:29:28,091
pool, a lock. Most web services are I-O bound.

434
00:29:28,740 --> 00:29:35,527
The interview is forty-five to sixty open-ended minutes. Seven steps: requirements, five minutes. Estimates,

435
00:29:35,527 --> 00:29:42,313
three to five. A-P-I, five. Data model, five. High-level design, ten. Deep dives, ten

436
00:29:42,313 --> 00:29:45,222
to fifteen. Trade-offs, three to five.

437
00:29:45,872 --> 00:29:51,239
